<ProjectLayout
  title="Private PKI and certificate lifecycle"
  description="Automated issuance and renewal of internal certificates via a private CA, with the trust chain correctly distributed across environments."
  domain="security"
  caseId="04"
>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Internal services, on private domains with no public exposure, needed real TLS — without relying
    on manually renewed self-signed certificates, a common source of silent expiration and browser
    warnings for anyone accessing these services day to day.
  </p>

  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <p>
    Establishing a <strong>private certificate authority</strong> in the cloud, with certificate issuance
    and renewal automated via orchestration tooling (in this case,{' '}
    <code>cert-manager</code> on Kubernetes) — the service only needs to declare that it needs a certificate;
    the entire lifecycle becomes automatic.
  </p>

  <h2>The point that matters most</h2>
  <p>
    The technical detail that required the most investigation, and generalizes to any private PKI:
    importing individual leaf certificates into a browser's or system's trust store does{' '}
    <em>not</em> establish trust for the chain as a whole — every new service generated a new untrusted-certificate
    warning, even though it was issued by the same CA. The correct fix is to import the <strong
      >CA's root certificate</strong
    > into the trust store, which resolves it for the entire chain at once. It's expected PKI behavior,
    but easy to get wrong in practice when you're not explicitly thinking in terms of the trust chain.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second, more environment-specific problem: in some browsers, DNS-over-HTTPS ignored the
    private network's internal resolver, causing name validation to fail even with the correct
    certificate — fixed by adjusting the DoH configuration to respect the private network's resolver
    for those domains.
  </p>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <p>
    Elimination of manual internal certificate expiration and TLS standardization across all private
    services, with a single configuration point for the entire infrastructure — the kind of decision
    that only needs to be thoroughly investigated once, then replicates with no additional effort.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>
